Z||000000||Deer_Slayer||02-14-2000||02:00 PM||||Yesterday i was watching the fishing shows on TNN.  During the commercials, there was one that only showed anSUV sittin in a suburban driveway.  There was a narrator giving a speech.  I don't remember all that he said but in went on the lines of "with its automatic transmission, some recklessly call it a truck.  Any real man knows a stationwagon when he sees one.  The only offroading this "car" will see is if backed over a flower bed."  And he went on at the end to say something like," If we accept this as an offroading vehicle, we should trade in our pants for skirts right now."  i just had to take off my hat to that guy.||||reg||6
Z||000001||Monkeyman||02-14-2000||04:26 PM||||I've always liked the Chevy/GMC Suburbans.  I used to have an '87.  Ambulance doors, light bar, 12" suspension lift, 4" body lift, custom tube bumpers, and 48" Terra tires.  WaaHoo!!  Absolutely worthless on ice, but you couldn't stop it in the snow, mud, grass, dirt, etc.  I got the chance to run over an old Yugo at a boneyard once.  What a rush!||||reg||1
Z||000002||HF6582||02-14-2000||05:01 PM||||A Yugo in the boneyard? That is an odd sight.<BR>I thought that every Yugo driver had to have at least 10 in his backyard to keep the one running!!!!!!!!!!<P>Scott||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cooter||02-14-2000||07:41 PM||||I never got into that SUV stuff. I have a  3/4 ton Suburban that I use to pull trailers with and haul stuff. It is basically an oversized 6000 pound station wagon, with enough room for 9 passengers and their luggage. It rides good, has good visibility and actually gets decent mileage-11 to 18 mpg, while pulling a loaded car trailer.||||reg||1
